# PhoneNr validation for Table_query

Companion notes for `SIM_PhoneNr_Validation_DEMO.xlsx`. The demo has the same explanation on its
Readme sheet — this file is here so you can copy the formulas as text.

Assumed layout (header row 1, data from row 2):

| Col | Header |
|---|---|
| C | PhoneNr |
| H | SIM_Country |
| T | IsPhoneValid |
| V | PhoneClean ← new helper column |

If your columns sit elsewhere, swap the letters and everything follows.

---

## Step 1 — add a `PhoneClean` helper column (V2)

Splitting normalisation out of the check keeps both formulas readable and avoids repeating the
`SUBSTITUTE` chain seven times. Hide the column afterwards if you don't want to see it.

```excel
=IF($C2="","",IF(LEFT(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(TRIM($C2&""),"+","")," ",""),"-",""),"(",""),")",""),".",""),CHAR(160),""),2)="00",MID(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(TRIM($C2&""),"+","")," ",""),"-",""),"(",""),")",""),".",""),CHAR(160),""),3,255),SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(TRIM($C2&""),"+","")," ",""),"-",""),"(",""),")",""),".",""),CHAR(160),"")))
```

Strips `+`, space, non-breaking space, `-`, `(`, `)`, `.` — then converts a leading `00`
international prefix to bare digits (`0040…` → `40…`).

## Step 2 — the check, in `IsPhoneValid` (T2)

```excel
=IF($C2="","",IF(NOT(ISNUMBER($V2+0)),"Invalid characters - digits only (+ ( ) - . and spaces are tolerated)",IF(LEN($V2)<8,"Too short - "&LEN($V2)&" digits (min 8)",IF(LEN($V2)>15,"Too long - "&LEN($V2)&" digits (max 15)",IF($H2="","SIM_Country is empty - cannot check the country code",IF(ISNA(MATCH($H2,tblCountries[Name],0)),"SIM_Country """&$H2&""" not found in tblCountries",IF(LEFT($V2,LEN(TEXT(INDEX(tblCountries[DialCode],MATCH($H2,tblCountries[Name],0)),"0")))<>TEXT(INDEX(tblCountries[DialCode],MATCH($H2,tblCountries[Name],0)),"0"),"Wrong country code - must start with +"&TEXT(INDEX(tblCountries[DialCode],MATCH($H2,tblCountries[Name],0)),"0")&" for "&$H2,"OK")))))))
```

Returns `""` when PhoneNr is empty, `"OK"` when valid, otherwise a specific message. Checks run in
this order: digits only → length 8–15 → country present → country known → dial code matches.

`INDEX`/`MATCH` rather than `XLOOKUP` so it works in Excel 2016 and 2019 too.

## Step 3 — conditional formatting

Select `$C$2:$C$2000`, then **Home → Conditional Formatting → New Rule → Use a formula**.
Add all three, in this order, and repeat the set for `$T$2:$T$2000`.

| # | Formula | Format | Stop If True |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `=AND($C2<>"", ISNUMBER(SEARCH("SIM_Country",$T2)))` | fill `FFEB9C`, font `9C5700` | ✔ |
| 2 | `=AND($C2<>"", $T2<>"", $T2<>"OK")` | fill `FFC7CE`, font `9C0006` bold | ✔ |
| 3 | `=$T2="OK"` | fill `C6EFCE`, font `006100` | |

Amber is deliberately *not* red: it means the number couldn't be checked because SIM_Country is
blank or unknown, so the fix belongs in the country cell or the Config sheet, not the phone number.

## Step 4 — Data Validation (optional popup)

Select `$C$2:$C$2000` → **Data → Data Validation → Allow: Custom**, tick *Ignore blank*:

```excel
=OR($C2="",AND(ISNUMBER(IF(LEFT(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(TRIM($C2&""),"+","")," ",""),"-",""),"(",""),")",""),".",""),CHAR(160),""),2)="00",MID(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(TRIM($C2&""),"+","")," ",""),"-",""),"(",""),")",""),".",""),CHAR(160),""),3,255),SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(TRIM($C2&""),"+","")," ",""),"-",""),"(",""),")",""),".",""),CHAR(160),""))+0),LEN(IF(LEFT(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(TRIM($C2&""),"+","")," ",""),"-",""),"(",""),")",""),".",""),CHAR(160),""),2)="00",MID(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(TRIM($C2&""),"+","")," ",""),"-",""),"(",""),")",""),".",""),CHAR(160),""),3,255),SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(TRIM($C2&""),"+","")," ",""),"-",""),"(",""),")",""),".",""),CHAR(160),"")))>=8,...))
```

It's long because it can't reuse `PhoneClean` — see below. Easiest route: **copy cell C2 from the
demo file and Paste Special → Validation** onto your own PhoneNr column.

Set **Error Alert → Style: Warning** (not Stop), so the user is told but can keep the value and fix
it later while the red fill keeps flagging it.

Also add a dropdown on SIM_Country (`Allow: List`, source `=Config!$A$2:$A$51`). A typo'd country
name is the most likely way this whole thing silently stops working.

## Step 5 — format the PhoneNr column as Text

Do this *before* any data goes in, or Excel strips the leading `+` and mangles long numbers.

---

## Three things that will bite you

**Data Validation can't read column T.** At the moment validation fires, T still holds the result
for the *previous* value — the sheet hasn't recalculated. So the DV formula has to carry its own
copy of the logic. Change a threshold and you must change it in both places, or just drop the DV
and rely on the colours.

**Data Validation doesn't fire on paste.** Or on fill-down, or from macros. That's Excel, not a bug
here. Conditional formatting re-evaluates on every recalculation, so treat the colours as the real
safety net and the popup as a convenience.

**Structured references are banned in DV and CF.** `tblCountries[Name]` works fine in the column T
formula but Excel rejects it inside a validation or conditional-formatting rule, which is why those
use `Config!$A$2:$A$51`. Those ranges are static — widen them if the country list grows.

## Known limits

- **Shared dial codes.** `+1` is the US, Canada and much of the Caribbean; `+7` is Russia and Kazakhstan. A US number validates cleanly as Canada. Separating them needs area-code rules, not dial codes.
- **Trunk zeros.** `+40 0712…` (dial code plus a national leading `0`) passes the dial-code test but isn't valid E.164. Tell me if your data has this pattern and it's a small addition.
- **8–15 is global.** Real national numbering plans are narrower and vary per country. Add MinLen/MaxLen columns to tblCountries if you need that precision.
- **`ISNUMBER(x+0)`** would accept an oddity like `1E5`. Irrelevant for phone data, but it's the one gap in the digits-only test.

## If you're on Excel 365 and want one formula instead of two

`LET` collapses Steps 1 and 2 into a single cell, no helper column:

```excel
=LET(
  raw,   $C2&"",
  c1,    SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(TRIM(raw),"+",""),"-","")," ",""),"(",""),")",""),".",""),CHAR(160),""),
  clean, IF(LEFT(c1,2)="00",MID(c1,3,255),c1),
  dial,  TEXT(IFERROR(XLOOKUP($H2,tblCountries[Name],tblCountries[DialCode]),""),"0"),
  IFS(
    raw="",                      "",
    NOT(ISNUMBER(clean+0)),      "Invalid characters - digits only",
    LEN(clean)<8,                "Too short - "&LEN(clean)&" digits (min 8)",
    LEN(clean)>15,               "Too long - "&LEN(clean)&" digits (max 15)",
    $H2="",                      "SIM_Country is empty",
    dial="",                     "SIM_Country """&$H2&""" not found in tblCountries",
    LEFT(clean,LEN(dial))<>dial, "Wrong country code - must start with +"&dial&" for "&$H2,
    TRUE,                        "OK"
  )
)
```

Same behaviour. The demo ships the `INDEX`/`MATCH` version because it also runs on older Excel.
